{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg45MqVfdejQX6CRLYgbG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I updated `reference/techniques/binary-reverse-engineering.md`. Nothing else was touched, and nothing was run against the GM.\n\n1. **Day by day:** I added a D15 \"Stripped\" entry. The new group was two ELFs built from the same C source, `-stripped.elf` and `-symbolized.elf`. The only new thing was a member tag inside the field name, `dNN_analysis_<qualifier>_<field>`. `sha256_1`/`_2` follow archive order (symbolized first), and `machine` came back as the integer `62`. Single solve went CORRECT 74 s after start with no wrong answers. Time Trial took 5.2 s, and Mastery (5 sets, 750 ids) took 9.35 s, both first try. D1–D14 are compressed to one short paragraph, so the file is 219 lines.\n2. **Variant axes:** I added a \"Member qualifier in the id\" axis. Its rule: route each id to the member whose file name carries the tag, and if names ever stop carrying it, treat the member without a symbol table as the stripped one. I also merged D15 into existing axes: 5 Mastery sets again, Time Trial no longer the same bytes as Mastery set1, set1 d01 stripped again, and machine asked as an integer for d15 but as text for d01 in the same contract.\n3. **Cues, conventions, pitfalls:** there's a new row for a pair of members that differ only by a name tag, and the integer-machine and format conventions now extend to D15. Two pitfalls are new: post the known ids first so they bank, and strip a tag off the front of an unknown field name before calling the field unknown. The note that the prompt's theme is never asked now covers D15's \"reconstruct the stripped functions\" (10th day running)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":940,"cache_read_input_tokens":64188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_in